**Synchronous:** Code executes line by line. Each line waits for the previous to complete.

```javascript
console.log('1');
const data = readFileSync('file.txt'); // Blocks until file is read
console.log('2');
// Output: 1, [file content], 2
```

**Asynchronous:** Code doesn't wait. Execution continues while operations happen in the background.

```javascript
console.log('1');
readFile('file.txt', (err, data) => {
  console.log('3');
});
console.log('2');
// Output: 1, 2, 3
```

**Why async matters:**

```javascript
// Sync - server handles ONE request at a time
app.get('/data', (req, res) => {
  const data = db.querySync(); // 100ms - blocks everything
  res.json(data);
});
// 10 requests = 1 second total

// Async - server handles MANY requests concurrently
app.get('/data', async (req, res) => {
  const data = await db.query(); // 100ms - but doesn't block
  res.json(data);
});
// 10 requests = ~100ms total (concurrent)
```

**Async patterns:**

```javascript
// Callbacks (old)
readFile('file.txt', (err, data) => { ... });

// Promises
readFile('file.txt').then(data => { ... });

// Async/await (modern)
const data = await readFile('file.txt');
```

**Key insight:** JavaScript is single-threaded. Sync operations block that thread. Async operations let the thread do other work while waiting for I/O (network, disk, etc.).

For servers: always async for I/O. For scripts: sync is fine if you don't need concurrency.

**Built-in (use this):**
```javascript
const flat = nested.flat(Infinity);
```

**Recursive (if you need custom behavior):**
```typescript
function flatten<T>(arr: (T | T[])[]): T[] {
  const result: T[] = [];

  for (const item of arr) {
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      result.push(...flatten(item));
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

**Iterative (no recursion):**
```typescript
function flatten<T>(arr: any[]): T[] {
  const stack = [...arr];
  const result: T[] = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const item = stack.pop();
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      stack.push(...item);
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result.reverse();
}
```

**With depth limit:**
```typescript
function flatten<T>(arr: any[], depth = 1): T[] {
  if (depth < 1) return arr.slice();

  return arr.reduce((acc, item) => {
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      acc.push(...flatten(item, depth - 1));
    } else {
      acc.push(item);
    }
    return acc;
  }, []);
}
```

Just use `.flat(Infinity)` unless you need specific depth control or older browser support.

**Rule:** Never concatenate user input into SQL strings. Use parameterized queries.

**Bad (vulnerable):**
```javascript
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = '${userId}'`;
db.query(query);
```

**Good (parameterized):**
```javascript
// Node postgres
await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [userId]);

// MySQL2
await db.execute('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [userId]);

// Prisma
await prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id: userId } });

// Knex
await knex('users').where('id', userId);
```

**ORMs handle this automatically:**
```typescript
// Prisma - safe by default
const user = await prisma.user.findFirst({
  where: { email: userInput }
});

// Drizzle - safe by default
const user = await db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.email, userInput));
```

**If you must use raw SQL:**
```typescript
// Prisma raw with params
await prisma.$queryRaw`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ${email}`;

// The template literal is safe - Prisma parameterizes it
```

**Additional defenses:**
- Input validation (but not as primary defense)
- Least-privilege database accounts
- WAF with SQL injection rules

Parameterized queries are the fix. Everything else is defense in depth.

Objects inherit directly from other objects via the prototype chain.

**How it works:**
```javascript
const animal = {
  speak() {
    console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound`);
  }
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.name = 'Rex';
dog.bark = function() { console.log('Woof!'); };

dog.speak(); // "Rex makes a sound" - inherited from animal
dog.bark();  // "Woof!" - own method
```

**The prototype chain:**
```javascript
dog.speak();
// 1. Check dog.speak - not found
// 2. Check dog.__proto__.speak (animal.speak) - found!
// 3. If not found, check animal.__proto__ (Object.prototype)
// 4. If not found, check Object.prototype.__proto__ (null) - undefined
```

**With constructor functions:**
```javascript
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
Animal.prototype.speak = function() {
  console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound`);
};

function Dog(name) {
  Animal.call(this, name); // Call parent constructor
}
Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype);
Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;
Dog.prototype.bark = function() { console.log('Woof!'); };
```

**With ES6 classes (syntactic sugar):**
```javascript
class Animal {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  speak() { console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound`); }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  bark() { console.log('Woof!'); }
}
```

Under the hood, classes use the same prototype chain. The `extends` keyword sets up the prototype relationship automatically.

